---
// The page-level attribution surface. Markup and styles only; it has no logic
// to put anywhere else. See docs/spec/attribution-surface.md.
---

<!--
  Bottom-left, and it stays there at every viewport.

  The page has four corners and three are spoken for: the header owns top-left,
  the desktop card owns bottom-right, and on a phone the full-bleed sheet owns
  the whole bottom edge whenever a card is open. Measured rather than argued —
  top-right lands 3211px² on the header caption at 375px, and bottom-left is
  100% behind the sheet on both phone viewports with a card open.

  ⚠️ The sheet occlusion is **accepted, not fixed**: occluded by a dismissible,
  non-modal overlay is not the same as conditionally rendered — the element is in
  the DOM and displayed on load, and the sheet is one gesture from dismissal. The
  alternative (re-homing into the header lockup below the breakpoint) is clear
  everywhere and costs a DOM re-home rather than a media query, which would give
  `(max-width: 700px), (max-height: 500px)` a **third** holder.
-->
<div class="pattr">
  <!--
    Google's own `poweredby.png`, vendored **unaltered** and served from this
    origin. Not hotlinked: this page makes zero third-party requests, and a
    hotlinked PNG would disclose every visitor's IP and referer to Google on a
    `noindex` site, to satisfy an obligation that has nothing to do with privacy.

    ⚠️ **62x30, measured** — the spec assumed 144x26, which was a guess nobody
    had checked.

    ⚠️ **It is dark on transparent** — 554 of its 613 opaque pixels — so on a
    page whose background is #1a1613 it is invisible, and Google publishes no
    light variant (`poweredby_lt.png` and its siblings all 404). Hence the plate
    underneath: the *image* is displayed exactly as Google serves it, which is
    what "do not change any of the Google marks" asks, and the surface it sits on
    is ours to choose.
  -->
  <!--
    ⚠️ **Not `loading="lazy"`**, which it was for about ten minutes. A lazy image
    loads when the browser decides it is needed, and this one's entire premise is
    that it is *always displayed* — deferring it is a smaller version of the
    hotlinking failure #104 rejected, where the obligation is met by something
    outside our control. It is 3.4 KB.
  -->
  <img class="pattr-mark" src="/poweredby-google.png" alt="powered by Google" width="62" height="30" />
  <!--
    The first page chrome on this site that is not inert. The surface sets itself
    `pointer-events: none` so drag-to-look-around passes straight through it; the
    anchor has to opt back out.
  -->
  <a class="pattr-link" href="/attribution">Attribution</a>
</div>

<style>
  .pattr {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    left: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    /* Above the shelf, below the card and the debug surfaces. */
    z-index: 5;
    /* Inert by default — the shelf is dragged through this corner. */
    pointer-events: none;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #6f6051;
  }

  /*
    The plate, and the reason it exists is in the markup above: the image is dark
    on transparent and this page is near-black. Deliberately the *quietest* light
    that keeps it legible rather than a white chip — Google's own constraint is
    that the graphic must not be the most prominent element on the page, and on a
    dark room with one lit bookcase a bright rectangle in the corner would be
    exactly that.
  */
  .pattr-mark {
    display: block;
    width: 62px;
    height: 30px;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.3rem;
    box-sizing: content-box;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    background: rgba(242, 232, 220, 0.72);
  }

  .pattr-link {
    /*
      44px, and it is the height of the whole row: WCAG 2.5.5's touch target,
      not the graphic, is what sets it — so shrinking the graphic buys width and
      nothing else.
    */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 0.25rem;
    color: #857361;
    /* The one thing here that is not inert. */
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  .pattr-link:hover,
  .pattr-link:focus-visible {
    color: #c9b8a4;
  }
</style>
